WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.207
Roll two dice. How likely is it that they add up to seven?

00:03.307 --> 00:07.483
You probably have a hunch. Let's build the answer from scratch, because the

00:07.583 --> 00:11.132
way we build it is the whole of probability theory in miniature.

00:12.216 --> 00:15.930
Three ideas: a sample space, events as subsets of it,

00:16.030 --> 00:19.457
and probability as a kind of mass spread over it.

00:20.550 --> 00:24.388
First, list everything that can happen. The first die shows one

00:24.488 --> 00:27.326
to six, the second die one to six, so there are

00:27.426 --> 00:31.264
thirty-six outcomes. This complete list is the sample space, S.

00:31.350 --> 00:35.038
An event is just a collection of outcomes, a subset of S. The

00:35.138 --> 00:39.635
event 'the total is seven' is these six cells, running along the diagonal.

00:39.733 --> 00:44.018
If the dice are fair, all thirty-six outcomes are equally likely.

00:44.118 --> 00:47.594
So the probability of the event is the share of cells

00:47.694 --> 00:50.630
it covers: six out of thirty-six, one in six.

00:51.716 --> 00:55.007
Here's a more useful way to think about it. Imagine

00:55.107 --> 00:58.729
one kilogram of sand, spread over the sample space. With

00:58.829 --> 01:02.718
fair dice, every cell gets the same pinch: one thirty-sixth.

01:02.816 --> 01:06.314
The probability of any event is simply how much sand sits on

01:06.414 --> 01:10.331
its cells. And the three axioms of probability are just three facts

01:10.431 --> 01:14.528
about sand. There's never a negative amount. All of it together weighs

01:14.628 --> 01:17.826
one. And if two events share no cells, the sand on both

01:17.926 --> 01:20.584
is the sand on one plus the sand on the other.

01:20.883 --> 01:24.455
Nothing requires equal pinches. If the dice were loaded, the

01:24.555 --> 01:27.883
sand would pile up unevenly, and counting cells would no

01:27.983 --> 01:31.923
longer work. But weighing sand still would. That's why probability

01:32.023 --> 01:35.229
is defined through these axioms, not through counting.

01:36.316 --> 01:40.375
Two events with no cells in common are called mutually exclusive. A

01:40.475 --> 01:43.975
total of seven, and a double: you can't roll both at once.

01:44.066 --> 01:47.191
So the chance of one or the other is just the sum:

01:47.291 --> 01:52.351
six thirty-sixths plus six thirty-sixths, one third. That's axiom three at work.

01:52.950 --> 01:57.238
And the chance of not rolling seven is everything else: one minus a

01:57.338 --> 02:01.757
sixth, five sixths. The complement always takes the rest of the sand.

02:02.850 --> 02:07.660
When events do overlap, simply adding would count the shared cells twice.

02:07.760 --> 02:10.821
How to fix that is a story for a later lecture.

02:12.916 --> 02:16.874
So: the sample space is everything that can happen. An event

02:16.974 --> 02:20.120
is a subset of it. And a probability is how much

02:20.220 --> 02:23.840
of the total mass that subset holds. Every rule we meet

02:23.940 --> 02:27.628
later is a consequence of those three axioms about mass.
